      "problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=8-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-6{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
